    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0028",
      "sequence": 40,
      "meaning": "Whether with good feeling or bad, in anger or even laziness, repeating the name brings auspiciousness in all ten directions. Remembering that name, I bow my head to Raghunātha and compose the story of Rāma’s virtues. He will correct my work in every way, for his grace never tires of showing grace. Rāma is the excellent master and I such a poor servant; looking only to his own side, the treasury of mercy will nourish me. Worldly custom and the Veda attest the way of a good lord: hearing a petition, he recognizes the petitioner’s love. Country folk and townspeople, the poor and eminent, learned and foolish, obscure and renowned, good and poor poets—every man and woman praises a king according to personal understanding. A saintly, wise and gentle king, an embodiment of God and supremely compassionate in this world, hears and respectfully honours everyone’s fine words, recognizing composition, devotion, reverence and purpose. This is merely an earthly king’s nature; the king of Kosala is the crown of all-knowing ones. Rāma is delighted by unalloyed love—who in the world is slower or more impure-minded than I? Merciful Rāma will nevertheless honour this foolish servant’s affection and aspiration: he made stones into watercraft and bears and monkeys into wise ministers. I too am called his—everyone says so—and Rāma bears the ridicule. My master is Sītā’s Lord; his servant is Tulsīdāsa.",
