    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0029",
      "sequence": 41,
      "meaning": "My boldness and fault are exceedingly great; hearing such sin, even hell wrinkles its nose. Yet, recognizing that my dread is only of myself, Rāma has never once remembered this failing, even in a dream. Listening and looking with the eyes of a pure heart, the Master commends my devotion and intention. While being told, a fault disappears and becomes good in his heart; Rāma is pleased when he knows what lies within a servant’s heart. The Lord does not retain in mind a wrong already done, but remembers a hundred times a kindness held in the heart. Sugrīva later committed the same misconduct for which Vālī was slain like game by a hunter. Vibhīṣaṇa’s action was also the same, yet Rāma never held it in his heart even in a dream. When they met Bharata, he honoured them and praised them in the royal assembly. The Lord stayed beneath the trees while the monkeys sat on branches, yet he made them equal to himself. Nowhere, says Tulsī, is there a master like Rāma, the treasury of gracious character. Rāma’s goodness makes everyone’s affairs good; if this is eternally true, then Tulsī’s too will become good. Having thus stated my own virtues and faults, I bow my head once more to everyone and describe Raghubara’s stainless fame, hearing which removes Kali’s stain.",
