    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0030",
      "sequence": 42,
      "meaning": "I shall recount the lovely dialogue and story that Yājñavalkya told the eminent sage Bharadvāja; may all good people listen with joy. Śiva composed this beautiful narrative and graciously told it to Umā. Śiva then gave it to Kākabhuśuṇḍi, recognizing him as a qualified devotee of Rāma. From him Yājñavalkya received it, and he in turn sang it to Bharadvāja. Those hearers and speakers were alike in character, even-sighted, and knew Hari’s play. By their knowledge they knew all three times as plainly as a myrobalan fruit held in the palm. Other wise devotees of Hari also tell, hear and understand it in many ways. I too heard this story from my teacher at Sūkarakṣetra, but in childhood I did not then understand it so, for I remained exceedingly unaware. The hearer and speaker were treasuries of knowledge and Rāma’s story is profound; how could I understand it—I, an inert, deluded creature gripped by Kali’s stain?",
